From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] bl2: clear .sbss too

GCC for RISC-V puts small zero-initialized variables in .sbss. The BL2
linker script collected only .bss* into the range bl2_entrypoint.S
clears, so .sbss landed after __BSS_END__ and kept whatever the SRAM
held. sd_fast.c (0004) relies on zero-initialized state: sd_wide decides
whether sd_fast_pll() and sd_fast_disable() touch the SD controller,
including on a UART boot, where sd_fast_enable() returns at once, and
sd_hs the divider when the High Speed switch fails.
---
 plat/cv181x/bl2/bl2.ld.S | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)
diff --git a/plat/cv181x/bl2/bl2.ld.S b/plat/cv181x/bl2/bl2.ld.S
index 232a7d8..06d182a 100644
--- a/plat/cv181x/bl2/bl2.ld.S
+++ b/plat/cv181x/bl2/bl2.ld.S
@@ -65,7 +65,9 @@ SECTIONS
         . = ALIGN(16);
         __BSS_START__ = .;
         *(SORT_BY_ALIGNMENT(.bss*))
+        *(SORT_BY_ALIGNMENT(.sbss*))
         *(COMMON)
+        *(.scommon)
         . = ALIGN(16);
         __BSS_END__ = .;
     } >RAM
